helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149276				0	id	Thousands Separator				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154380				0	id	Depending on your language setting, you can use a comma or a period as a thousands separator. You can also use the separator to reduce the size of the number that is displayed by a multiple of 1000.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154905				0	id	Number Format				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150822				0	id	Format Code				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147264				0	id	15000 as 15,000				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151223				0	id	#,###				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154935				0	id	16000 as 16				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153961				0	id	#,				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154836				0	id	Including Text in Number Format Codes				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398				0	id	Text and Numbers				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154224				0	id	To include text in a number format that is applied to a cell containing numbers, place a double quotation mark (") in front of and behind the text, or a backslash (\\) before a single character. For example, enter \<emph\>#.# "meters"\</emph\> to display "3.5 meters" or \<emph\>#.# \\m\</emph\> to display "3.5 m".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148979				0	id	Text and Text				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153338				0	id	To include text in a number format that is applied to a cell that might contain text, enclose the text by double quotation marks (" "), and then add an at sign (@). For example, enter \<emph\>"Total for "@\</emph\> to display "Total for December".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154330				0	id	Spaces				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156294				0	id	To use a character to define the width of a space in a number format, type an underscore ( _ ) followed by the character. The width of the space varies according to the width of the character that you choose. For example, \<emph\>_M\</emph\> creates a wider space than \<emph\>_i\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155994				0	id	Warna				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156423				0	id	To set the color of a section of a number format code, insert one of the following color names in square brackets [ ]:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154630				0	id	CYAN				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148676				0	id	GREEN				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123				0	id	BLACK				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149167				0	id	BLUE				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3158407				0	id	MAGENTA				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149560				0	id	RED				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147502				0	id	WHITE				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	id	YELLOW				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147435				0	id	Conditions				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148575				0	id	Conditional Brackets				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155312				0	id	You can define a number format so that it only applies when the condition that you specify is met. Conditions are enclosed by square brackets [ ].				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159179				0	id	You can use any combination of numbers and the <, <=, >, >=, = and <> operators.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159196				0	id	For example, if you want to apply different colors to different temperature data, enter:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150872				0	id	[BLUE][<0]#,0 "°C";[RED][>30]#,0 "°C";[BLACK]#,0 "°C"				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870				0	id	All temperatures below zero are blue, temperatures between 0 and 30 °C are black, and temperatures higher than 30 °C are red.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154833				0	id	Positive and Negative Numbers				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147295				0	id	To define a number format that adds a different text to a number depending on if the number is positive, negative, or equal to zero, use the following format:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727				0	id	"plus" 0;"minus" 0;"null" 0				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149260				0	id	Percentages and Scientific Notation				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147218				0	id	Percentages				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151168				0	id	To display numbers as percentages, add the percent sign (%) to the number format.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156005				0	id	Scientific Notation				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3146923				0	id	Scientific notation lets you write very large numbers or very small fractions in a compact form. For example, in scientific notation, 650000 is written as 6.5 x 10^5, and 0.000065 as 6.5 x 10^-5. In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\>, these numbers are written as 6.5E+5 and 6.5E-5, respectively. To create a number format that displays numbers using scientific notation, enter a # or 0, and then one of the following codes E-, E+, e- or e+.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159080				0	id	Number Format Codes of Currency Formats				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147318				0	id	The default currency format for the cells in your spreadsheet is determined by the regional setting of your operating system. If you want, you can apply a custom currency symbol to a cell. For example, enter #,##0.00 € to display 4.50 € (Euros).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150032				0	id	You can also specify the locale setting for the currency by entering the locale code for the country after the symbol. For example, [$€-407] represents Euros in Germany. To view the locale code for a country, select the country in the \<emph\>Language\</emph\> list on the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab of the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157309				0	id	Date and Time Formats				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153740				0	id	Date Formats				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152791	38			0	id	To display days, months and years, use the following number format codes. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id610980				0	id	Not all format codes give meaningful results for all languages.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152376				0	id	Format				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159130				0	id	Format Code				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147380				0	id	Month as 3.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3146928				0	id	M				20181231 09:50:43
