helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	tit				0	id	Pivot Table				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150448				0	id	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; introduction\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>DataPilot, see pivot table function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150448				0	id	\<variable id=\"datapilot\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot.xhp\" name=\"Pivot Table\"\>Pivot Table\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	par_id3156024				0	id	The \<emph\>pivot table\</emph\> (formerly known as \<emph\>DataPilot\</emph\>) allows you to combine, compare, and analyze large amounts of data. You can view different summaries of the source data, you can display the details of areas of interest, and you can create reports.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot.xhp	0	help	par_id3145069				0	id	A table that has been created as a \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12090000.xhp\" name=\"pivot table\"\>pivot table\</link\> is an interactive table. Data can be arranged, rearranged or summarized according to different points of view.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	tit				0	id	Editing Pivot Tables				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148663				0	id	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; editing tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing;pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148663				0	id	\<variable id=\"datapilot_edittable\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot_edittable.xhp\" name=\"Editing Pivot Tables\"\>Editing Pivot Tables\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868				0	id	Click one of the buttons in the pivot table and hold the mouse button down. A special symbol will appear next to the mouse pointer.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786				0	id	By dragging the button to a different position in the same row you can alter the order of the columns. If you drag a button to the left edge of the table into the row headings area, you can change a column into a row.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id1648915				0	id	In the Pivot Table dialog, you can drag a button to the \<emph\>Page Fields\</emph\> area to create a button and a listbox on top of the pivot table. The listbox can be used to filter the pivot table by the contents of the selected item. You can use drag-and-drop within the pivot table to use another page field as a filter.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434				0	id	To remove a button from the table, just drag it out of the pivot table. Release the mouse button when the mouse pointer positioned within the sheet has become a 'not allowed' icon. The button is deleted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3156442				0	id	To edit the pivot table, click a cell inside the pivot table and open the context menu. In the context menu you find the command \<emph\>Edit Layout\</emph\>, which displays the \<emph\>Pivot Table\</emph\> dialog for the current pivot table.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id2666096				0	id	In the pivot table, you can use drag-and-drop or cut/paste commands to rearrange the order of data fields.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id266609688				0	id	You can assign custom display names to fields, field members, subtotals (with some restrictions), and grand totals inside pivot tables. A custom display name is assigned to an item by overwriting the original name with another name.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	tit				0	id	Automatically Calculating Series				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	id	\<bookmark_value\>series; calculating\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>calculating; series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>linear series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>growth series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>date series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>powers of 2 calculations\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; filling automatically\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>automatic cell filling\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>AutoFill function\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>filling;cells, automatically\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769				0	id	\<variable id=\"calc_series\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\" name=\"Automatically Calculating Series\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A8				0	id	You can automatically fill cells with data with the AutoFill command or the Series command.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D3				0	id	Using AutoFill				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D7				0	id	AutoFill automatically generates a data series based on a defined pattern.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	id	On a sheet, click in a cell, and type a number.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CB				0	id	Click in another cell and then click back in the cell where you typed the number.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	id	Drag the fill handle in the bottom right corner of the cell across the cells that you want to fill, and release the mouse button.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801				0	id	The cells are filled with ascending numbers.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EE				0	id	To quickly create a list of consecutive days, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>Monday\</item\> in a cell, and drag the fill handle.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id9720145				0	id	Hold down \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	id	If you select two or more adjacent cells that contain different numbers, and drag, the remaining cells are filled with the arithmetic pattern that is recognized in the numbers. The AutoFill function also recognizes customized lists that are defined under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</item\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10737				0	id	You can double-click the fill handle to automatically fill all empty columns of the current data block. For example, first enter Jan into A1 and drag the fill handle down to A12 to get the twelve months in the first column. Now enter some values into B1 and C1. Select those two cells, and double-click the fill handle. This fills automatically the data block B1:C12.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10713				0	id	Using a Defined Series				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	id	Select the cell range in the sheet that you want to fill.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	id	Pilih \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Sunting - Pilih Semua\</item\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10716				0	id	Select the parameters for the series. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	id	If you select a \<emph\>linear\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>added\</emph\> to each consecutive number in the series to create the next value.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073C				0	id	If you select a \<emph\>growth\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>multiplied\</emph\> by each consecutive number to create the next value.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10747				0	id	If you select a \<emph\>date\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is added to the time unit that you specify.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3159173				0	id	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	tit				0	id	Menggunakan Format Otomatis pada Tabel				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155132				0	id	\<bookmark_value\>tables; AutoFormat function\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>defining;AutoFormat function for tables\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>AutoFormat function\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formats; automatically formatting spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>automatic formatting in spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sheets;AutoFormat function\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155132				0	id	\<variable id=\"autoformat\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/autoformat.xhp\" name=\"Using AutoFormat for Tables\"\>Applying Automatic Formatting to a Selected Cell Range\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3149401				0	id	You can use the AutoFormat feature to quickly apply a format to a sheet or a selected cell range.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN10702				0	id	To Apply an AutoFormat to a Sheet or Selected Cell Range				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CE				0	id	Select the cells, including the column and row headers, that you want to format.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D5				0	id	Pilih \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - OtoFormat\</item\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3151242				0	id	To select which properties to include in an AutoFormat, click \<emph\>More\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN10715				0	id	Klik \<emph\>Oke\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN1075D				0	id	The format is applied to the selected range of cells.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3149210				0	id	If you do not see any change in color of the cell contents, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>View - Value Highlighting\</item\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3155379				0	id	To Define an AutoFormat for Spreadsheets				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3148868				0	id	You can define a new AutoFormat that is available to all spreadsheets.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3152985				0	id	Format a sheet.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3145384				0	id	Pilih \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Sunting - Pilih Semua\</item\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3153815				0	id	Pilih \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - OtoFormat\</item\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A9				0	id	Klik \<emph\>Tambah\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN10760				0	id	In the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> box of the \<emph\>Add AutoFormat\</emph\> dialog, enter a name for the format.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN107C3				0	id	Klik \<emph\>Oke\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3159203				0	id	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05110000.xhp\" name=\"Format - OtoFormat\"\>Format - OtoFormat\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	tit				0	id	Importing and Exporting Text Files				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153726				0	id	\<bookmark_value\>csv files;formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas; importing/exporting as csv files\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>exporting;formulas as csv files\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>importing;csv files with formulas\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153726				0	id	\<variable id=\"csv_formula\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/csv_formula.xhp\" name=\"Importing and Exporting Text Files\"\>Importing and Exporting CSV Text Files with Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149402				0	id	Comma separated values (CSV) files are text files that contain the cell contents of a single sheet. Commas, semicolons, or other characters can be used as the field delimiters between the cells. Text strings are put in quotation marks, numbers are written without quotation marks.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150715				0	id	To Import a CSV File				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153709				0	id	Choose \<emph\>File - Open\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3155445				0	id	In the \<emph\>File type\</emph\> field, select the format "Text CSV". Select the file and click \<emph\>Open\</emph\>. When a file has the .csv extension, the file type is automatically recognized.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565				0	id	You will see the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Text Import\</item\> dialog. Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149255				0	id	If the csv file contains formulas, but you want to import the results of those formulas, then choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</emph\> and clear the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> check box.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154022				0	id	To Export Formulas and Values as CSV Files				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150342				0	id	Click the sheet to be written as a csv file.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3166423				0	id	If you want to export the formulas as formulas, for example, in the form =SUM(A1:B5), proceed as follows:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3155111				0	id	Choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150200				0	id	Under \<emph\>Display\</emph\>, mark the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> check box. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3154484				0	id	If you want to export the calculation results instead of the formulas, do not mark \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3148702				0	id	Choose \<emph\>File - Save as\</emph\>. You will see the \<emph\>Save as\</emph\> dialog.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912				0	id	In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File type\</item\> field select the format "Text CSV".				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3157978				0	id	Enter a name and click \<emph\>Save\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3152869				0	id	From the \<emph\>Export of text files\</emph\> dialog that appears, select the character set and the field and text delimiters for the data to be exported, and confirm with \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3146781				0	id	If the numbers use commas as decimal separators or thousands separators, do not select the comma as the field delimiter. If the text contains double quotation marks, you must select the single quotation mark as separator.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150050				0	id	If necessary, after you have saved, clear the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> check box to see the calculated results in the table again.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153487				0	id	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060100.xhp\" name=\"Spreadsheet - View\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153008				0	id	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000207.xhp\" name=\"Export text files\"\>Export text files\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3155595				0	id	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000208.xhp\" name=\"Import text files\"\>Import text files\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	tit				0	id	Inserting and Editing Comments				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153968				0	id	\<bookmark_value\>comments; on cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells;comments\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>remarks on cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting;comments on cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>viewing;comments on cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>displaying; comments\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153968				0	id	\<variable id=\"note_insert\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/note_insert.xhp\" name=\"Inserting and Editing Comments\"\>Inserting and Editing Comments\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3150440				0	id	You can assign a comment to each cell by choosing \<link href=\"text/shared/01/04050000.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Comment\"\>\<emph\>Insert - Comment\</emph\>\</link\>. The comment is indicated by a small red square, the comment indicator, in the cell.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3145750				0	id	The comment is visible whenever the mouse pointer is over the cell, provided you have activated \<emph\>Help - Tips\</emph\> or - \<emph\>Extended Tips\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	id	When you select the cell, you can choose \<emph\>Show Comment\</emph\> from the context menu of the cell. Doing so keeps the comment visible until you deactivate the \<emph\>Show Comment\</emph\> command from the same context menu.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958				0	id	To edit a permanently visible comment, just click in it. If you delete the entire text of the comment, the comment itself is deleted.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_idN10699				0	id	Move or resize each comment as you like.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_idN1069D				0	id	Format each comment by specifying background color, transparency, border style, and text alignment. Choose the commands from the context menu of the comment.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3144764				0	id	To show or hide the comment indicator, choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</emph\> and mark or unmark the \<emph\>Comment indicator\</emph\> check box.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	id	To display a help tip for a selected cell, use \<emph\>Data - Validity - Input Help\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	id	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/04050000.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Comment\"\>Insert - Comment\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	tit				0	id	Menamai Sel				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	id	\<bookmark_value\>cells; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>names; defining for cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>values; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>constants definition\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>variables; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell ranges; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>defining;names for cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>addressing; by defined names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell names; defining/addressing\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>references; by defined names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>allowed cell names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>renaming;cells\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434				0	id	\<variable id=\"value_with_name\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/value_with_name.xhp\" name=\"Menamai Sel\"\>Menamai Sel\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id4391918				0	id	Allowed names				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id2129581				0	id	Names in Calc can contain letters, numeric characters, and some special characters. Names must start with a letter or an underline character.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id1120029				0	id	Allowed special characters:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3362224				0	id	underline (_) 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4891506				0	id	period (.) - allowed within a name, but not as first or last character				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id2816553				0	id	blank ( ) - allowed within a name, but not as first or last character, and not for a cell range				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id328989				0	id	Names must not be the same as cell references. For example, the name A1 is invalid because A1 is a cell reference to the top left cell.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id32898987				0	id	Names must not start with the letter R followed by a number. See the ADDRESS function for more information.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4769737				0	id	Names for cell ranges must not include blanks. Blanks are allowed within names for single cells, sheets and documents.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id1226233				0	id	Naming cells and formulas				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id5489364				0	id	A good way of making the references to cells and cell ranges in formulas legible is to give the ranges names. For example, you can name the range A1:B2 \<emph\>Start\</emph\>. You can then write a formula such as "=SUM(Start)". Even after you insert or delete rows or columns, $[officename] still correctly assigns the ranges identified by name. Range names must not contain any spaces.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id953398				0	id	For example, it is much easier to read a formula for sales tax if you can write "= Amount * Tax_rate" instead of "= A5 * B12". In this case, you would name cell A5 "Amount" and cell B12 "Tax_rate."				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4889675				0	id	Use the \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialog to define names for formulas or parts of formulas you need more often. In order to specify range names,				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153954				0	id	Select a cell or range of cells, then choose \<emph\>Insert - Names - Define\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialog appears.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283				0	id	Type the name of the selected area in the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> field. Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>. The newly defined name appears in the list below. Click OK to close the dialog.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id5774101				0	id	You can also name other cell ranges in this dialog by entering the name in the field and then selecting the respective cells.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154942				0	id	If you type the name in a formula, after the first few characters entered you will see the entire name as a tip.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510				0	id	Press the Enter key in order to accept the name from the tip.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	id	If more than one name starts with the same characters, you can scroll through all the names using the Tab key.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153711				0	id	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04070100.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Names - Define\"\>Insert - Names - Define\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	tit				0	id	Calculating With Formulas				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155411				0	id	\<bookmark_value\>formulas;calculating with\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; with formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>examples;formula calculation\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155411				0	id	\<variable id=\"formulas\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formulas.xhp\" name=\"Calculating With Formulas\"\>Calculating With Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3156281				0	id	All formulas begin with an equals sign. The formulas can contain numbers, text, arithmetic operators, logic operators, or functions.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	id	Remember that the basic arithmetic operators (+, -, *, /) can be used in formulas using the "Multiplication and Division before Addition and Subtraction" rule. Instead of writing =SUM(A1:B1) you can write =A1+B1.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	id	Parentheses can also be used. The result of the formula =(1+2)*3 produces a different result than =1+2*3.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3156285				0	id	Here are a few examples of $[officename] Calc formulas:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3154015				0	id	=A1+10				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146972				0	id	Displays the contents of cell A1 plus 10.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3145643				0	id	=A1*16%				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3154255				0	id	Displays 16% of the contents of A1.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146917				0	id	=A1 * A2				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146315				0	id	Displays the result of the multiplication of A1 and A2.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3154022				0	id	=ROUND(A1;1)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3150363				0	id	Displays the contents of cell A1 rounded to one decimal place.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3150209				0	id	=EFFECTIVE(5%;12)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3150883				0	id	Calculates the effective interest for 5% annual nominal interest with 12 payments a year.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146114				0	id	=B8-SUM(B10:B14)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486				0	id	Calculates B8 minus the sum of the cells B10 to B14.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3152890				0	id	=SUM(B8;SUM(B10:B14))				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3159171				0	id	Calculates the sum of cells B10 to B14 and adds the value to B8.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3150109				0	id	It is also possible to nest functions in formulas, as shown in the example. You can also nest functions within functions. The Function Wizard assists you with nested functions.				20181231 09:50:43
